WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.549
This right here is Apple’s new $1300 base
13” MacBook Pro that finally comes with

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:10.920
a quad-core processor, and we’re gonna connect
it to the same eGPU that we previously connected

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.039
to the top-spec 15” MacBook Pro, where we
concluded that adding an eGPU wasn’t worth

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:17.039
it.

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:19.980
But as you’ll see in this video, we’re
completely changing our mind because this

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.589
$2000 setup works like a dream.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:29.359
So for the eGPU system, we’re using the
$300 Razer Core X with a $400 Radeon Vega

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:35.449
64 graphics card, and combined with the new
$1299 base 13” MacBook Pro, that makes for

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:44.510
a total of $2,000 for this setup compared
to $3,549 for the top-spec 15” MacBook Pro.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.859
Now of course, the 15” model is gonna get
it’s high performance wherever it goes,

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:53.030
while the 13” needs to be connected to the
eGPU to get the performance we’re about

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:58.059
to show you, but a lot of people actually
prefer the portability of the 13” for when

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:00.820
they’re on the go, so this setup makes a
lot of sense.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:07.390
And even without an eGPU, the new $1300 13”
base MacBook Pro actually performs very well,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.930
and we’ll be comparing it directly to the
$1800 13” Pro very soon, so if you’re

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.240
not already subscribed, do that now.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.840
And without further ado, let’s get into
performance.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.700
In Geekbench 4’s graphics test, we can see
that we now get a lot more graphics performance

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.619
than the 15” MacBook Pro now that we’re
using the eGPU.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:30.530
And in Unigine Heaven, a gaming benchmark,
we’re now getting almost double the gaming

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:35.280
performance compared to the 15” model with
Vega 20 graphics, so if you’re wanting to

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.560
game on a MacBook Pro, this is the perfect
one for an eGPU.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.760
Now let’s move onto some video editing tests
in Final Cut Pro, which do a great job of

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.729
utilizing both graphics and processor performance.

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:51.539
Let’s start with the BruceX benchmark, which
almost purely uses graphics, and as you can

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:57.329
see, the eGPU setup is actually finishing
faster than the $3500 MacBook Pro!

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:02.700
And now on video stabilization of 4K footage,
it’s also finishing slightly faster than

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:08.780
15” Pro, once again, and it’s actually
5x faster than the integrated graphics alone.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.170
So those tests were mainly using graphics
performance, which we know is faster with

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.430
the eGPU, but now let’s do some tasks that
also use up a good amount of processing performance

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.430
as well.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:24.040
I’d first like to mention that the top-spec
15” model gets an 8-core i9 CPU which is

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:30.010
obviously much much faster than the 1.4GHz
quad-core in the $1300 base model, but let’s

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.629
give it a chance, starting with editing common
4K footage with a couple of effects and LUTs

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:35.629
added to it.

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:39.220
This is basically what most YouTubers are
gonna be editing if they’re shooting in

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.220
4K.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.409
And we see here that the base MacBook Pro
with the eGPU is actually not too far off

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:50.340
from the 15”, so this is really impressive
for paying only $2000 compared to $3500.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.569
And interestingly, when we previously added
the eGPU to that same 15” Pro, it actually

00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:01.849
took longer for some reason, around the same
time as the 13” Pro with the eGPU, so we’re

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:03.400
getting a lot more out of it.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:09.970
Now let’s move onto some heavy duty footage,
C200 4K RAW at 60 frames per second, we actually

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:16.069
got a massive improvement with the eGPU connected,
exporting around 3 times as fast, and not

00:03:16.069 --> 00:03:18.440
too far behind the 15” MacBook Pro.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:23.769
And not only that, but when we playback the
timeline, the eGPU triples the amount of FPS

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:28.430
we get while editing, almost as much as the
high-end model alone, so it actually becomes

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.320
bearable to edit this footage.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.750
And now exporting 4.5K R3D RAW, footage that’s
extremely processor heavy, we’re actually

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.420
seeing another massive improvement with the
eGPU.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:43.159
We’re still not quite at the 15” Pro’s
level, but it beats waiting over 40 minutes

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:44.310
to export this clip.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:48.460
Those were only a few limited video editing
tests, but we’re extremely surprised that

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:53.760
we’re getting this much performance out
of the base $1300 MacBook Pro with a $700

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:54.760
eGPU.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.150
And the biggest difference was in tasks that
use mainly the graphics, like gaming and graphics

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:03.269
rendering, so if you’re doing work that’s
heavily dependent on graphics performance,

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:05.890
than this $2000 system is for you!

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.750
But the most impressive thing was how we also
saw a major difference in processor heavy

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:14.159
tasks as well, and the reason for that is
because the quad-core processor was being

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:19.060
held back by the limited performance of the
integrated graphics, and the eGPU gave the

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.770
CPU all the room it needed to perform at its
best.

00:04:21.770 --> 00:04:26.990
And need I remind you that this MacBook only
has 8GB of RAM and it’s performing this

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:34.030
well, so if you’re gonna upgrade anything,
upgrade the RAM, absolutely don’t pay $300

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.060
for the i7 upgrade.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:43.970
So at only $1300, I’m officially crowning
the 2019 13” base MacBook Pro, the best

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:48.670
MacBook you can get if you’re gonna use
an eGPU, and also as the overall best value

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.520
MacBook out of any MacBook we’ve seen in
years.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.860
We’ve also tested a lot of eGPU systems
and this Razer Core X enclosure combined with

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.810
the Radeon Vega 64 is the best bang for the
buck eGPU system we’ve seen.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:06.780
We also have AMD’s brand new Radeon 5700
XT in the office, but we won’t be able to

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.120
test it until we get driver support, hopefully
in macOS Catalina this fall.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:18.230
And if you’re wondering how this $1300 MacBook
Pro compares to the $1800 2.4GHz model, that

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.970
video is coming very soon, so if you’re
not already subscribed, click that circle

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.150
above so you don’t miss out on that video.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.520
Tap like if you enjoyed this video, and check
out one of those two videos right there.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:32.060
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

